    "blocker": null,
    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "A council wants to find the mean income of all residents in a city. It surveys $90$ residents who live in wealthy waterfront neighbourhoods.\nExplain why the sample may be unrepresentative.",
        "solution_text": "The population is all residents in the city.\nThe sample includes only residents of wealthy waterfront neighbourhoods and leaves out residents elsewhere.\nThese residents may have higher incomes than other residents, so the sample may overestimate the city’s mean income.\n**This is selection bias**."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A school wants to know the percentage of its $840$ students who have maths as their favourite subject. It surveys only the $28$ students in its Year 12 Maths Extension class.\nDescribe the sampling bias.",
        "solution_text": "The population is all $840$ students at the school.\nOnly students in the Year 12 Maths Extension class are included.\nThese students may be more interested in maths than students in other classes and year levels.\nThe sample may overestimate the percentage who have maths as their favourite subject.\n**This is selection bias**."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A council wants to know farmers’ opinions about a new road. It asks $3$ farmers from a community of $640$.\nExplain why this sample may be unrepresentative.",
        "solution_text": "The population is all $640$ farmers in the community.\nOnly $3$ farmers are surveyed.\nTheir opinions may differ from those of the other $637$ farmers, and the sample may miss much of the variety of opinions.\n**The sample size is too small to give a reliable picture of the community’s views**."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Researchers invite adults in a town to volunteer for a study about eating habits. Only $75$ health-conscious adults volunteer.\nExplain why the sample may be unrepresentative.",
        "solution_text": "The population is all adults in the town.\nThe sample includes only the health-conscious adults who volunteered.\nTheir eating habits may differ from those of adults who did not volunteer.\n**This is volunteer bias**."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A library wants to know all local residents’ views about its opening hours. It posts a survey on its social-media page, and $160$ people choose to reply.\nExplain why the sample may be unrepresentative.",
        "solution_text": "The population is all local residents.\nResidents who do not use the library’s social-media page or do not see the survey are left out.\nOnly people who choose to reply are included. People with strong views about the opening hours may be more likely to answer.\n**The sample risks selection bias and volunteer bias**, because respondents’ views may differ from those of other residents."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "An online newspaper asks readers whether they enjoy answering surveys. Of the $2400$ people who choose to reply, nearly all say yes.\nThe newspaper claims that people generally enjoy answering surveys because it received so many replies.\nExplain why this conclusion may not be valid.",
        "solution_text": "The population in the claim is people generally.\nThe sample includes only readers who see the survey and choose to reply.\nPeople who dislike answering surveys may be less likely to respond, so their views may be missing.\n**The conclusion may not be valid because of volunteer bias and possible selection bias**.\nA large number of replies does not show that the sample represents people who did not reply."
      }
    ],
